            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 26-year-old male admitted on 13/04/2003 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point with positive rebound. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis. Abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. Given the clinical and laboratory findings, the patient underwent laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 13/04/2003 without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperatively, the patient received intravenous fluids, analgesics, and prophylactic antibiotics. Wound healing was satisfactory. Vital signs remained stable throughout the stay. The patient was mobilised early and discharged on 15/04/2003 in good general condition. Please continue outpatient follow-up and wound monitoring. Do not hesitate to contact me should any questions arise.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
